  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 242 AM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft via longwave ridge is anticipated on Sunday to \nkick off this forecast period. Said ridge, centered over the western \nGreat Lakes region, should propagate fairly quickly to the east over \nthe course of the day Monday. Upper-level winds become zonal through \nMonday. Guidance currently projects a second ridge to develop on \nTuesday across the the Plains region, settling the flow pattern back \nto southwesterly through the middle of next week. This, all ahead of \nan upper-level low that is expected to move across the SW CONUS \nthrough Wednesday. Guidance does outline a brief shortwave early \nMonday which should move out of the County Warning Area (CWA) by the \ntime the evening comes around. Not much is expected to come from \nthis trough passage other that a wind shift. A weak surface low is \nlikely to form over eastern Colorado Sunday, moving into western \nKansas Monday. This feature also should not do much other than \npressure changes and wind shifts. Ensemble guidance is beginning to \npick up a stronger surface low that may set up near the Tri-State \narea late Wednesday.\n\nForecast guidance anticipates conditions to be quite dry across the \nCWA through early Wednesday. 5% or less PoPs (Probability of \nPrecipitation) are favored throughout the region. This may not last \nthough as an upper-level low, mentioned in the above section, paired \nwith low-pressure at the surface could bring the next decent \nrainfall chances to our region late Wednesday. Guidance is currently \nnot very confident on widespread precipitation, but a general 15-25% \nPoPs is forecasted at this point in time. Surface analysis/QPF \ndoesn't show much accumulation for our area right now; nonetheless, \nstill worth monitoring as this event draws closer.\n\nMoving on to high/low temperatures for the coverage area, a slight \nwarmup is expected across the board as we rebound from well-below \naverage highs. Temps in the low/mid 70s are likely Sunday and \nMonday, with a slight uptick to mid/upper 70s Tuesday then upper \n70s/low 80s possible next Wednesday. With respect to overnight lows, \nSunday morning is likely to be the coldest, with temps expected to \napproach the mid 30s. Lows will then gradually warm up above the mid \n30s, with upper 30s/low 40s expected Monday morning. Tuesday and \nWednesday should continue this warming trend with low/mid 40s and \nthen upper 40s/low 50s expected, respectively. \n\nAs for fire weather concerns, the potential for critical fire \nconditions will be low, but not zero. Relative humidity (RH) \nvalues are likely to reach the mid-teens (14-15%) in our \neastern Colorado counties on Sunday. Meanwhile, surface winds \nover these areas should sit around 15-20 mph from the north, \ngusting up to 30 mph. Going forward, RH values are anticipated \nas low as the upper teens west of US-385 Monday and Tuesday, but\nwinds should be light and variable; thus, the fire weather \noutlook likely will be milder post-Sunday. For the rest of the \nCWA, RH values in the 20-40% range is to be expected with \ngenerally 5-15 mph winds. Winds will initially be from the south\nMonday, shifting to the north and east with the shortwave, then\nsettling back to more of a southerly direction. Gusts are not \nlikely to exceed 25 mph during this forecast period everywhere \neast of the Colorado/Kansas state line.",
